nues to call for CPI of 3.8% in October, up from 3.7% in for September. Mortgage rates were flat last week, with the average 30-year fixed-rate mortgage still at 7.40%, according to FreddieMac. Taking a deeper dive into performance so far in 2026, a leading industrialized global stock market index, the ETF EFA, is up 7% year to date, while the leading emerging market ETF (EEM) is up 15%. U.S. growth stocks are up 2% so far in 2026 when looking at ETF IWF, and value stocks (IWD) are up 21%. Crude oil continues to be volatile. On Friday, oil was at about $99 per barrel, up 71% for the year. In other asset classes for the year, AGG bonds are down 5%, gold is down 4%, and Bitcoin is down 7%. The U.S. dollar is up 4%, tracking DXY. The VIX Volatility Index was up to about 20 on Friday, in line with its historical average. Turning to sector performance, the list from first to worst so far in 2026 is Energy (+49%), Information Technology (+31%), Healthcare (+14%), Consumer Staples (+12%), Materials (+11%), Industrial (+8%), Real Estate (+4%), Financial (+1%), Utilities (-2%), Communication Services (-4%), and Consumer Discretionary (-5).\n\nArgus• 5 days ago \n- ### Nike: Moat Rating Lowered to Narrow; Fair Value Estimate Cut by 44%; Shares Remain Undervalued\n\nSince its founding in 1964, Nike has become the world's largest sports performance and lifestyle merchandiser. Footwear is its largest category, accounting for about two-thirds of sales. It also sells large amounts of apparel (about 30% of sales) and equipment (5%). Key performance categories include basketball, running, and football (soccer). Brands include Nike, Jordan (premium athletic footwear and clothing), ACG (outdoor), NikeSkims (women’s athleisure), and Converse (casual footwear). Nike sells products worldwide through company-owned stores, franchised stores (including about 5,500 in China), and third-party retailers. The firm also operates e-commerce platforms in more than 40 countries. It outsources nearly all production to contract manufacturers in more than 30 countries. \n\nMorningstar• 8 days ago \n- ### Nike: Moat Rating Lowered to Narrow; Fair Value Estimate Cut by 44%; Shares Remain Undervalued\n\nSince its founding in 1964, Nike has become the world's largest sports performance and lifestyle merchandiser. Footwear is its largest category, accounting for about two-thirds of sales. It also sells large amounts of apparel (about 30% of sales) and equipment (5%). Key performance categories include basketball, running, and football (soccer). Brands include Nike, Jordan (premium athletic footwear and clothing), ACG (outdoor), NikeSkims (women’s athleisure), and Converse (casual footwear). Nike sells products worldwide through company-owned stores, franchised stores (including about 5,500 in China), and third-party retailers. The firm also operates e-commerce platforms in more than 40 countries. It outsources nearly all production to contract manufacturers in more than 30 countries. \n\nMorningstar• 8 days ago","title":"NIKE, Inc. (NKE) Stock Price, News, Quote & History - Yahoo Finance","url":"https://finance.yahoo.com/quote/NKE/"},{"author":null,"id":"https://finance.yahoo.com/quote/LULU/","text":"lululemon athletica inc. (LULU) Stock Price, News, Quote & History - Yahoo Finance\n\n# lululemon athletica inc. (LULU)\n\n 99.01 -5.05 (-4.85%) \n\n At close: October 23 at 4:00:01 PM EDT \n\n 99.16 +0.15 (+0.16%) \n\nAfter hours: October 23 at 7:39:49 PM EDT \n\n## Trading disclosure \n\n### 1.97%\n\n## What's happening with LULU?\n\n34m ago\n\nLululemon faces a weakening North American business, shifting customer preferences, and reduced fiscal 2026 guidance. The latest selloff appeared broader than company-specific, while leadership, product, and board changes aim to support a turnaround. The stock is down 4.85% in the current regular session as of October 25, 2026.\n\n What notable LULU insider transactions have been filed recently? What's behind LULU's 4.85% drop t